// Info Wave W5 page 5/9 — "ge refrigerator not cooling" (1,500/mo KD0). Brand sniper.
// GE: coils, evap fan, defrost, main control board, sealed system; largely symptom-
// diagnosed (few no-cool codes). Food-safety block (USDA/FDA). Safe DIY only.
// umbrella<->sniper links. $89.

import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ServiceHero from '../../../components/ServiceHero.astro';
import { MAIN_PHONE } from '../../../data/branches';
import { BRANCH_LOCATIONS_GEO_NEUTRAL } from '../../../lib/build-location-array';

const phone = MAIN_PHONE;
const phoneRaw = MAIN_PHONE.replace(/\D/g, '');
const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/refrigerator-repair/ge-not-cooling/";

const title = "GE Refrigerator Not Cooling? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = "GE refrigerator not cooling? Usually dirty coils, the evaporator fan, the defrost system, or the main board. GE is diagnosed by symptom, not codes. Safe checks, food-safety timing, when to call.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my GE refrigerator not cooling?",
    a: "On a GE that runs but won't cool, the usual causes are dirty condenser coils, a failed evaporator fan (which shows as 'freezer cold, fridge warm'), a defrost system that's iced over the evaporator, and — on newer units — the main control board that manages compressor cycling, fans, and defrost timing. A locked compressor or sealed-system fault is the serious end. Most GE refrigerators are diagnosed by symptom rather than an error code, so a GE that won't cool usually just runs warm with nothing on the display. Cleaning the coils and checking airflow are yours to do; the board and sealed system are a tech job."
  },
  {
    q: "Does a GE refrigerator show a code when it's not cooling?",
    a: "Usually not. Most GE refrigerators are diagnosed by symptom, not by a digital no-cool code — a GE that won't cool typically just runs warm with nothing on the display, and you find the fault by testing the cooling parts (coils, fan, defrost, board, sealed system). If your GE shows no code, that doesn't mean the control is dead; it's simply how GE refrigerators work. Some newer models with a main control board can show a temperature or diagnostic indicator, but the diagnosis order is the same: coils and airflow first, then the fan, defrost, board, and sealed system."
  },
  {
    q: "My GE freezer is cold but the fridge is warm — what's wrong?",
    a: "That split — cold freezer, warm fridge — is the classic evaporator fan failure. The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge; when it fails or frost from a defrost fault jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. On GE, a failed defrost system or a main board that's stopped running the defrost cycle is a common root cause of the frost that jams the fan. Both are fixable, tech-level repairs inside the cabinet. Keep the doors closed while you wait — the fridge food is on roughly a four-hour clock."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a GE refrigerator that's not cooling myself?",
    a: "Some of it. Vacuum the condenser coils (behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate), make sure interior vents aren't blocked, confirm the doors seal and the temperature is set right, pull the fridge a few inches off the wall to shed heat, and give it a power-cycle. What you should not do is open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor, or replace the main control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are a tech's job. If it stays warm after a coil clean, call the same day."
  },
  {
    q: "How long is food safe in a GE that stopped cooling?",
    a: "Per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door kept closed, and a full freezer holds a safe temperature for roughly 48 hours (24 hours if half-full) if you keep it shut. Perishable food — meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, leftovers — held above 40°F for 2 hours or more should be thrown out. When in doubt, throw it out, and never taste food to check. That 4-hour fridge window is why a no-cool refrigerator is a same-day call — keep the doors closed and get a tech out before it closes."
  }
];

