---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/ge-not-cooling"
  eyebrow="GE Refrigerator Not Cooling · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="GE Refrigerator Not Cooling? Start With the Coils and Fan"
  subtitle="Most GE no-cool calls are dirty coils, the evaporator fan, the defrost system, or the main board — and GE is diagnosed by symptom, not codes. Here's what to check and how long your food stays safe."
  heroImageTitle="GE Refrigerator Not Cooling Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A running-but-warm GE is usually the coils, the fan, or defrost.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a GE refrigerator runs but won't cool, the causes are a short list: dirty condenser coils, a failed evaporator fan ("freezer cold, fridge warm"), a defrost system iced over the evaporator, and — on newer units — the main control board. A locked compressor or sealed-system fault is the serious end. Most GE refrigerators are diagnosed by symptom, not by a code, so a GE that won't cool usually just runs warm with nothing on the display — that's normal, not a dead control. Cleaning the coils and checking airflow are safe to do yourself; the board and sealed system are a tech job. Treat a no-cool fridge as a same-day call — your food is on a clock. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Food is on a clock:</strong> per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door closed. Keep the doors shut, move critical items to a cooler with ice if you can, and get a tech out before that window closes. Full food-safety details are in the section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 SYMPTOM-DIAGNOSED -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · GE is diagnosed by symptom, not a code</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">No code on the display? That's normal for GE.</h2>

    <p>Unlike LG or Samsung, most GE refrigerators don't display a no-cool error code. A GE that won't cool typically just runs warm with nothing on the panel, and you find the fault by testing the cooling parts in order rather than reading a code. If your GE shows no code, that's simply how GE refrigerators work — it doesn't mean the control is dead.</p>

    <p>So the diagnosis is symptom-led: is the freezer cold while the fridge is warm (evaporator fan), is there frost on the back panel (defrost), are the coils caked in dust (condenser), or is the compressor running without getting cold (sealed system)? Our techs read those symptoms the way GE intends the units to be serviced, which is faster than chasing a code that isn't there. For the full picture across brands, see the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a GE stops cooling</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils (check first)</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust and pet hair can't shed the compressor's heat, so the fridge slowly loses cooling. The most common homeowner-fixable cause, and worse in an LA summer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan — "freezer cold, fridge warm"</h3>
      <p>The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge. When it fails or frost jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. A tech job inside the cabinet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater, sensor, or control lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass and cooling drops. The tell is a wall of frost behind the freezer's back panel — and it's often what jams the evaporator fan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Main control board (newer units)</h3>
      <p>On newer GE refrigerators the main board manages compressor cycling, the fans, and defrost timing. A failed board can stop cooling in ways that mimic several parts, so we test it rather than guess. Tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor runs but doesn't cool or won't start, the sealed system is the fault — a gauges-and-tech diagnosis, and the higher-ticket end of the list.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils</strong> behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate. This alone fixes a lot of gradual no-cool cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the interior vents</strong> so food isn't blocking airflow between compartments.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal and temperature setting,</strong> and confirm the setting wasn't bumped warm.</li>
      <li><strong>Give it room and a power-cycle</strong> — pull the fridge off the wall, unplug a minute, restart.</li>
      <li><strong>Note the symptoms</strong> — freezer cold while fridge is warm, or frost on the back panel — so we can pinpoint the fault faster.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> coils, vents, seal, temperature, and a power-cycle are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor, or replace the main control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FOOD SAFETY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Heat-wave food safety — when to call today</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A warm fridge is a clock. Here are the numbers.</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator that's stopped cooling is a race against the clock, and in an LA heat wave that clock runs faster. Per USDA and FDA guidance:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Refrigerator: about 4 hours</strong> of safe food with the door kept closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Freezer: roughly 48 hours full, 24 hours half-full,</strong> if the door stays shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Above 40°F for 2 hours or more → discard it.</strong> That covers meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, and leftovers.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out</strong> — and never taste food to decide. Frozen food can be safely refrozen only if it still has ice crystals or is at 40°F or below.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>That's why a no-cool GE is a same-day call. Keep the doors closed, move the critical items to a cooler with ice, and get a tech out before the four-hour fridge window closes. We answer phones 24/7 and run same-day across most of the area. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Coils clean, still warm? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the coils are clean, the vents are clear, and the GE still won't cool, the fault is inside — the evaporator fan, the defrost system, the main board, or the compressor and sealed system. Our techs diagnose GE by symptom, test each in order, and carry the common GE fan motors and defrost parts for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide (all brands), and if it's the fridge warm while the freezer's fine, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/ge-refrigerator-repair/">GE refrigerator repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/refrigerator-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">GE refrigerator not cooling: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>GE refrigerator not cooling? Call today — food is on a clock.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

